# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://api.nobelprize.org/2.1/> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q38104> ,
              <https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/lists/all-nobel-prizes-in-physics/> ,
              <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Bragg> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Prizes and selection. On 2026-10-04 the Nobel Prize API was queried for all Nobel Prizes in Physics (nobelPrizeCategory=phy, sorted by year) and for all physics laureates (see Queries). It returned 125 prize years, 1901–2025, and 229 laureates. Six years have no laureates: 1916, 1931, 1934, 1940, 1941 and 1942; the API's topMotivation and NobelPrize.org's summary page for each say \"No Nobel Prize was awarded this year\". They are left out. Each of the other 119 years became one card, with the year (the API's awardYear, the year the prize is for) on the front, tagged zxx as a number in no language. The 2026 prize is announced on 6 October 2026 (NobelPrize.org press release \"The 2026 Nobel Prize announcements\", fetched 2026-10-04), two days after the deck was made, so the deck ends with 2025."@en ,
                 "Back: English. The laureates of each year in the order the API lists them, joined with \", \" and \" and \". The English names are the API's knownName.en, the names under which the Nobel Foundation lists the laureates, with initials and their spacing kept as given (J.J. Thomson, but \"E. M. Purcell\", as the API and Wikidata write it). Six names were changed, each documented under Quality control: (a) the API writes \"Il´ja M. Frank\", with an acute accent (´) standing for the Russian soft sign, and \"Nicolay G. Basov\"; the cards write \"Ilya M. Frank\" and \"Nikolay G. Basov\", combining the usual English spelling of the first name (Wikidata's English labels \"Ilya Frank\" and \"Nikolay Basov\") with the API's initial, as for the other laureates of 1958 and 1964; (b) the knighthood \"Sir\", which the API gives only for Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman and Nevill F. Mott, is left out (the cards write the API's name without the title; for Raman this is also his English Wikidata label, while for Mott the English form \"Nevill F. Mott\" is the API's name without \"Sir\", Wikidata's English label being \"Nevill Francis Mott\"), as the API leaves it out for the other knighted laureates such as J.J. Thomson, William Bragg and James Chadwick; \"Lord Rayleigh\", the name under which he is known as a scientist (a Wikidata alias in English and Swedish; the Swedish Wikipedia: \"Som vetenskapsman är han känd som Lord Rayleigh\"), is kept in both languages as a fixed name, not a title before a name; (c) \"Alexei Abrikosov\" is written \"Alexei A. Abrikosov\", with the initial of the patronymic in the API's fullName (\"Alexei Alexeyevich Abrikosov\"; also a Wikidata alias and NobelPrize.org's form), so that all three 2003 laureates have an initial; (d) the typographic apostrophe in \"Anne L’Huillier\" is written as the straight apostrophe of Wikidata's label \"Anne L'Huillier\" and of the deck's other names and notes (\"Gerardus 't Hooft\")."@en ,
                 "Back: Swedish. The same laureates joined with \", \" and \" och \". Each Swedish name is the API's Swedish name (knownName.se), which equals the English name except for Russian names, where the API gives the Swedish transliteration (Pavel A. Tjerenkov, Ilja M. Frank, Aleksandr M. Prochorov, Pjotr Kapitsa, Vitalij Ginzburg). Eight Swedish names were changed, each to a form that is a Wikidata Swedish label or alias (query 2) or the Swedish Wikipedia article title (query 3), or that adds an initial from the API's Swedish fullName: \"Igor Jevgenjevitj Tamm\" (full patronymic) to \"Igor J. Tamm\" (sv alias), so that all three 1958 laureates have an initial; \"Nicolaj G. Basov\" to \"Nikolaj G. Basov\" (sv alias and Swedish Wikipedia title; Swedish transliteration writes k); \"Zhores Alferov\" (the English transliteration) to \"Zjores Alfjorov\" (sv label and Swedish Wikipedia title); \"Alexei Abrikosov\" to \"Alexej A. Abrikosov\" (sv label and Swedish Wikipedia title); \"Vitalij Ginzburg\" to \"Vitalij L. Ginzburg\" (the initial of \"Vitalij Lazarevitj Ginzburg\", the API's Swedish fullName; sv alias \"Vitalij L Ginzburg\"), so that all three 2003 laureates have an initial; \"Sir Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman\" and \"Sir Nevill F. Mott\" to \"Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman\" and \"Nevill F. Mott\" (sv labels and Swedish Wikipedia titles; see step 3, and Swedish writes titles before names in lower case); and \"Anne L’Huillier\" to \"Anne L'Huillier\" (sv label; see step 3). Other names that Swedish writes the same as English were kept as the API gives them, including where the Swedish Wikipedia title is a fuller or shorter variant (e.g. Wilhelm Röntgen, Paul Dirac)."@en ,
                 "Second source for every laureate. Wikidata was queried (query 1) for every item with P166 award received = Q38104 (Nobel Prize in Physics), with the year of its P585 point in time qualifier and the statement's rank. All 230 laureate-years in the API (229 laureates; John Bardeen twice) were matched by the Wikidata id the API itself gives (laureate.wikidata.id), and every one has Q38104 with the API's year, after one correction: the API links Pierre Agostini (2023) to Q122921025, which is now a redirect to Q57020342 (merged duplicate); the deck's checks use Q57020342. Wikidata also gives the prize, with no year, to Q629583, Sheldon Cooper, a fictional character in a television series; it is not a laureate and is ignored."@en ,
                 "Third source. NobelPrize.org's summary page for each year 1901–2025 was fetched (one request per second, script fetch_summary.py) and compared with the cards: the laureates, the six years without an award and the late awards agree with the API. Its award sentence is quoted in each card's evidence with the motivations elided. NobelPrize.org is copyrighted and used for verification only."@en ,
                 "Notes. Each card's backNote says in a few words what the prize was for. The notes shorten the motivation in the API (the Nobel Foundation's citation, which the API makes available under CC0 in English and Swedish; for 2021 alone the API has no Swedish motivation, so that year's Swedish note is translated from the English one); many follow its wording closely, with the leading \"for\" dropped and a word or two changed or left out, and the Swedish notes likewise follow the Swedish motivation where it reads naturally; where a year's laureates were cited for different work, the note names each part (e.g. 1978: \"Kapitsa: low-temperature physics; Penzias and Wilson: discovering the cosmic microwave background.\"). Swedish technical terms were compared with the Swedish labels of the corresponding Wikidata items (query 4) and the eponymous terms written as those labels write them (Zeemaneffekten, Starkeffekten, Comptonspridning, Ramanspridning, Tjerenkovstrålning, Josephsoneffekt); the one exception is \"Mössbauereffekten\", whose Wikidata sv label (and Swedish Wikipedia title) is hyphenated, \"Mössbauer-effekten\", but which is written closed like the deck's other eponymous terms, as Q918552's sv alias \"Mössbauereffekten\" (a redirect on Swedish Wikipedia) writes it, as listed under the Wikidata source, in the definite form where the sentence needs it. The notes also give, from the API and confirmed by NobelPrize.org: the nine prizes awarded a year late (1914, 1917, 1918, 1921, 1924, 1925, 1928, 1932, 1943; the API's dateAwarded falls in the next year; for each but 1914 the summary page says the laureate \"received his Nobel Prize one year later\" or, for 1925, \"their\"; for 1914, whose NobelPrize.org pages do not mention it, the Swedish Wikipedia article on Max von Laue confirms it: \"Nobelpriset tillkännagavs först hösten 1915\"); Marie Curie's Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1911; and John Bardeen's two physics prizes (1956, 1972). Four notes give a father-and-son link from Wikidata P40 child, confirmed by Wikipedia: William and Lawrence Bragg (1915, who shared the prize), J.J. Thomson (1906) and G.P. Thomson (1937), Niels Bohr (1922) and Aage Bohr (1975), Manne Siegbahn (1924) and Kai Siegbahn (1981). In Swedish the late award is \"Priset tilldelades honom ett år senare\" (the award decision, as in the API's dateAwarded; \"dem\" for 1925)."@en ,
                 "Machine checks. Every card carried Wikidata checks run by the builder against live Wikidata: for every laureate, P166 includes Q38104; the English and Swedish name on the card is a Wikidata label or alias in that language (or the \"mul\" label) where Wikidata has that form; and the facts behind the notes' family links (P40) and Marie Curie's chemistry prize (P166 Q44585). The checks read only truthy property values and cannot see the P585 year qualifier, so the link between year and laureate is verified by query 1 and NobelPrize.org, not on every build. Name checks are left out where Wikidata does not have the Nobel Foundation's form (often an initialled form such as \"Albert A. Michelson\"); those names were confirmed against the API and NobelPrize.org's summary pages and are listed in Quality control. The deck was then validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators (node scripts/validate_sources.ts nobel-prize-in-physics)."@en ,
                 "Study direction. Front to back only: John Bardeen is on two cards (1956 and 1972), several backs are long lists of names, and a note on what the prize was for belongs with the year."@en ,
                 "Reproduction. The cards were generated from the saved API and Wikidata responses by a script (gen_cards.py) that applies the name decisions in steps 3 and 4 and no other transformation, joins the names, takes the notes from a table written by hand (notes.py), writes each card's evidence from the saved responses and NobelPrize.org pages, and adds the Wikidata checks. The scripts and raw responses are kept in the authoring agent's scratch directory, not in the repository; the queries and URLs below are enough to fetch the same data again and the rules above to rebuild the cards. The changes made after review (Quality control rounds 1–3: names in steps 3 and 4, and five notes) were made to the dossier directly, with the Wikidata labels and Swedish Wikipedia texts behind them fetched on 2026-10-04 (queries 15 and 16)."@en ,
                 "Selection: All 119 years from 1901 to 2025 in which the Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded, one card per year, with every laureate of that year: the complete set, nothing chosen. Left out: the six years with no award (1916, 1931, 1934, 1940, 1941, 1942), since a card for them would have no laureate to learn; and the 2026 prize, which is announced on 6 October 2026, after this deck was made on 4 October 2026. The year is the prize year, not the year of the award decision: the 1921 prize, awarded to Albert Einstein in 1922, is on the card 1921. Items that Wikidata also credits with the prize but that are not laureates (a fictional character) are not on the cards. The notes say what each prize was for in a few words; the laureates' nationalities and affiliations are not given, as many laureates had several."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from two sources whose terms allow that: the Nobel Prize API, whose terms say the service is \"free to use according to the Creative Commons Zero (CC0) license\", and Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0. The selection (every prize year with an award), the laureates and their order, the English and most Swedish names, the late awards and the multiple laureates come from the API, cross-checked with Wikidata; the changed Swedish names, the English spellings \"Ilya\" and \"Nikolay\", Raman's English name without \"Sir\" and the family links come from Wikidata, while Mott's English \"Nevill F. Mott\" is the API's name with \"Sir\" left out, and the added initials in \"Alexei A. Abrikosov\" and \"Vitalij L. Ginzburg\" from the API's own full names (\"Ilya M. Frank\" and \"Nikolay G. Basov\" combine Wikidata's spelling of the first name with the API's initial). The notes shorten the Nobel Foundation's motivations as the API gives them in English and Swedish, often closely following their wording; the API provides the motivations under CC0 too. Two sources were used for verification only and contributed nothing but confirmation of facts: NobelPrize.org's pages (copyright Nobel Prize Outreach: \"You may not reproduce, distribute ... or otherwise use the Content without prior written permission\"), read to confirm what the open sources give — the evidence quotes each year's one-sentence award statement from that year's summary page with the motivations elided (a short quotation to show what was checked; the same facts are CC0 from the same maker through the API, and the quotes are in the provenance report, not on the cards), but no text, selection or data field was taken, which also keeps clear of the EU sui generis database right in the site as a whole; and Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0), which confirms four family relations and the late award of the 1914 prize, facts, with nothing copied. The API terms add conditions for applications: not to alter the data or mislead users — the deck changes six English and eight Swedish names (transliterations, the title \"Sir\", initials and an apostrophe), each documented in the method and under Quality control — and to credit the API as the data source, which the deck does. They also say applications may not use Nobel Prize Outreach's name or trademarks \"in their titles or logos, or in any way that implies endorsement\", and NobelPrize.org's terms list \"Nobel Prize®\" among the Nobel Foundation's trademarks. The deck's title, \"Nobel Prize in Physics\" / \"Nobelpriset i fysik\", uses the prize's name only to say what the deck is about (descriptive use), with no logo, medal or other Nobel graphics, and its description says it is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Nobel Foundation or the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences. As for the literature and peace decks, whether that satisfies the API terms' \"titles\" clause is an open question, raised in Quality control rounds 3 and 6 and not yet settled; the decision will be recorded here; the fallback is a title without the prize's name, such as \"Physics laureates 1901–2025\" / \"Fysikpristagare 1901–2025\"."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Completeness and correctness against the sources; agreement between the Nobel Prize API, Wikidata and NobelPrize.org; Wikidata machine checks; Swedish names and terms"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 119 cards."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The API's 125 prize years (119 with an award, 6 without) and 230 laureate-years (229 laureates, John Bardeen twice) were matched one by one against Wikidata's P166 Q38104 statements with their P585 years and against NobelPrize.org's summary page for every year. The laureates and years agree in all three. The six years without an award and the eight late awards agree between the API and NobelPrize.org. All 626 Wikidata checks passed in the build (2026-10-04). The P166 checks confirm that each laureate received the prize but cannot see the year; each card's year rests on the API, NobelPrize.org and query 1."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 119 cards, against the Nobel Prize API, Wikidata (P166 Q38104 with P585) and the 2026 announcement date."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors found: every year's laureates match Wikidata's P166/P585 statements and the API, all notes are accurate, and ending at 2025 is right (the 2026 physics prize is announced on 6 October 2026). Three suggestions: the late award of the 1914 prize, a more precise 1919 note, and the name styles on the 2003 card. All three were applied."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 119 cards, the title, description and keywords; Swedish names and terms against Wikidata sv labels, sv.wikipedia and the API's Swedish motivations."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Language tags are clean (fronts zxx, backs and notes en+sv, no untagged text). Two warnings: the Swedish backs kept the capitalised English title \"Sir\" for Raman and Mott. Suggestions on name consistency (\"Sir\" in English, 2003 initials, \"E. M. Purcell\", apostrophes, \"Nicolay\") and on four Swedish notes. Both warnings and most suggestions were applied; \"Lord Rayleigh\" and \"E. M. Purcell\" were kept and documented."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every source's licence page, the deck licence, the evidence (16 cards spot-checked), the method, queries and licensing text."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The recorded licences are right and the deck can be CC0. One error: the description, method and licensing said the notes were in our own words and did not copy the motivations, whereas many closely follow them. Also the open trademark-in-title question, an inaccurate claim about \"Ilya M. Frank\", bare output paths in the recorded commands, and two forms of the same Swedish terms. The claims, the licensing text, the paths and the term forms were corrected; the title question stays open."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The 10 cards changed in round 3 and every third card in dossier order (48 cards): laureate order, names, dates and motivations against the Nobel Prize API; every Wikidata check re-run against all 229 laureate items; NobelPrize.org summary pages for 10 years; the method, sources, licensing text and build outputs."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No card errors; all checks pass, the API still returns 125 prize years matching the fronts, and the round 1–3 fixes are in place. One documentation warning (the method and licensing said Mott's English name without \"Sir\" came from Wikidata) and two suggestions (say that 2021 has no Swedish motivation; \"förtunnade gaser\" in the 2001 note). All three were verified against live sources and applied. The trademark-in-title question stays open, as logged in round 3."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The card changed in round 4 (2001) and every third card in dossier order (40 cards, 1902–2025) against the live Nobel Prize API (laureates, order, English and Swedish names, dateAwarded, motivations); every Wikidata check on those cards re-run; NobelPrize.org's 2001 summary and the 2026 announcement dates; language tags, description, keywords, licence decision and method."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors; round 4's three fixes are in place, all sampled checks pass and the API still returns 125 prize years with 119 matching fronts (physics 2026 is announced on 6 October at the earliest, after the deck's cut-off). Two documentation suggestions, both verified against live sources and applied: the Wikidata source named the sv alias \"Bose–Einstein-kondensation\" instead of the label \"Bose–Einstein-kondensat\" the 2001 note uses, and the 2001 card's evidence did not show the Swedish motivation behind its Swedish note. The trademark-in-title question stays open, as logged in round 3."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 119 cards against the live Nobel Prize API and NobelPrize.org (laureates, prize years, late awards, family links, the 2025 prize and the 2026 announcement dates); round 5's fixes; language tags, Swedish names and terms; title, description, keywords, study direction and the open licensing question."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No factual or language-tag errors in the 119 cards; the cut-off at 2025 is right (physics 2026 is announced on 6 October) and round 5's fixes are in place. One release blocker and two Swedish style suggestions. The trademark-in-title question was verified as still open (the API terms, fetched 2026-10-04, still bar Nobel Prize Outreach's trademarks \"in their titles or logos\"); it is re-raised here and left open. Both style suggestions were verified and applied: \"Mössbauereffekten\" is now written closed like the deck's other eponymous terms, and the 2020 Swedish note's Penrose part is a noun phrase following the Swedish motivation."@en .

<https://api.nobelprize.org/2.1/>
    dcterms:title "Nobel Prize API 2.1 (api.nobelprize.org): Nobel Prizes and laureates in physics (nobelPrizes and laureates endpoints)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Nobel Prize Outreach" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Every API response's meta block links the terms (\"terms\": \"https://www.nobelprize.org/about/terms-of-use-for-api-nobelprize-org-and-data-nobelprize-org/\", \"license\": \"…#licence\"), which say: \"The Services are free to use according to the Creative Commons Zero (CC0) license. In case of conflicting terms or other inconsistencies, these Terms shall prevail.\" They add: \"Applications that use the Services should not deliberately alter or censor the data, or deliberately mislead the end users\" and \"Applications and other products and services may not use Nobel Prize Outreach’s name, logos, or other trademarks in their titles or logos, or in any way that implies endorsement, sponsorship, or false association with Nobel Prize Outreach or any other association within the Nobel Foundation’s sphere. However, you are encouraged to cite the Services as the data source\"."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q38104>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing: \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/lists/all-nobel-prizes-in-physics/>
    dcterms:title "NobelPrize.org: the summary page of every Nobel Prize in Physics year and seven laureate pages" ;
    dcterms:creator "Nobel Prize Outreach" ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.nobelprize.org/about/terms-of-use/: \"Everything, including but not limited to the articles, texts, photographs, images, data, files, audio and video clips, illustrations, designs, educational games and documentation in whole or in part (the “Content”) which appears on the Site is either proprietary to Nobel Prize Outreach or used in accordance with applicable law or third party consents. You may not reproduce, distribute, display, transmit, modify, perform, adapt, generate derivative works or otherwise use the Content without prior written permission […]\"; and \"you may not use the Nobel Foundation trademarks without prior written permission from the Nobel Foundation\", which lists \"Nobel Prize®\" among them."@en .

<https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Bragg>
    dcterms:title "Wikipedia (English and Swedish): the articles Lawrence Bragg, George Paget Thomson, Aage Bohr and Kai Siegbahn, and the Swedish articles Max von Laue and John Strutt, 3:e baron Rayleigh" ;
